SCST +caw-abort-reclaim.6: hand a PR registrant to the new session of an I_T nexus, and keep the
old session's commands inside PREEMPT AND ABORT's reach.

A persistent-reservation registration belongs to the I_T nexus, which iSCSI names by initiator
name and ISID; SPC keeps it across the loss of the nexus and the session's reinstatement. When an
initiator logs in again with the same ISID after iscsi-scst has already started shutting the old
session down, the login is a new session, and scst_pr_init_tgt_dev() left the registrant on the
old session's per-device state (it only assigns a registrant that no tgt_dev holds). The
registrant moved only when the old tgt_dev was freed (scst_pr_clear_tgt_dev() from
scst_free_session(), after the old session's refcount reached zero), so between the new session's
creation and that free every command of the registered nexus was refused RESERVATION CONFLICT
(measured on the MXFS lab target, 2026-10-23: the five refused writes of a 4 s path cut all fell
6-14 ms after the login).

Part 1, scst_pr_init_tgt_dev(): the new tgt_dev takes the registrant under dev_pr_mutex when the
tgt_dev holding it belongs to a session that is no longer SCST_SESS_SPH_READY. Two READY sessions
never trade it.

Part 2, scst_pr_abort_reg(): moving the registrant alone would put the old session's commands out
of PREEMPT AND ABORT's reach, because the abort was queued only on reg->tgt_dev's session, and the
old session may still hold writes it admitted while it held the registrant. The new
scst_pr_abort_nexus_sessions() also queues SCST_PR_ABORT_ALL on every other session of the
registrant's nexus (same TransportID and relative target port) on the device whose refcount is
not yet zero. A tgt_dev leaves dev_tgt_dev_list only in scst_free_session(), after its session's
refcount reached zero, so every session of the nexus that still holds a command is found. The
sessions are pinned with percpu_ref_tryget() under dev_lock and aborted after it is dropped
(scst_rx_mgmt_fn_lun(SCST_NON_ATOMIC) may sleep); +caw-abort-reclaim.5 already admits a
core-originated PR_ABORT_ALL on a session in SCST_SESS_SPH_SHUTDOWN.

Both parts print one PRINT_INFO line when they act (not trace-mask controlled), so a run's host
kernel log shows each handoff and each extra abort.

Applied to /src/scst (the tree the installed modules are built from). Installing it is a reload of
SCST on the lab host: every rig VM is powered off first, the target is rebuilt with
scripts/scst_setup.sh afterwards, and tests/scst_pr_bounds_check.sh and
tests/scst_pr_abort_shutdown_race.sh must pass on the installed .6.

